---
/**
 * Why the library exists, and who built it — not the pitch (that is the landing
 * page's job, §5.5), the personal reasoning behind it.
 */
import { localeFromPath } from '../i18n/routing';
import { useTranslations } from '../i18n/ui';
import { site } from '../site';

const t = useTranslations(localeFromPath(Astro.url.pathname));
---

<section class="about" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    {/*
      The dummy this page is signed by, leaning on the text rather than beside it. Empty and
      first: a background carries the drawing, so nothing is announced to a screen reader and
      nothing is fetched on the windows that do not draw it, and a float only pushes what
      follows it. The rule below carries the measurements.
    */}
    <div class="mascot" data-dummy></div>

    <h2 id="about-heading">{t('about.heading')}</h2>

    <p>{t('about.origin')}</p>
    <p>{t('about.philosophy')}</p>

    <p>{t('about.linksIntro')}</p>
    <ul class="links">
        <li>
            <a href={site.repository} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {t('about.links.library')}
                <span class="visually-hidden"> {t('state.newTab')}</span>
            </a>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href={site.siteRepository} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {t('about.links.site')}
                <span class="visually-hidden"> {t('state.newTab')}</span>
            </a>
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href={site.library.nuget} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                {t('about.links.nuget')}
                <span class="visually-hidden"> {t('state.newTab')}</span>
            </a>
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<style>
    .about {
        max-width: 56rem;
    }

    /*
     * THE DUMMY ON THIS PAGE, and the one place on the site where a drawing sits against the
     * text rather than clear of it (§5.8). This page shows nothing — it is one person saying
     * why the library exists — so there is no figure for a drawing to compete with, and the
     * page it signs is the one about the person who drew the conclusion.
     *
     * WHY THE TEXT FOLLOWS HIS LEAN, and why that is the whole idea. Set beside the prose in
     * a rectangle, a leaning figure reads as a picture that happens to be there. Given the
     * text's own right edge, he reads as something the page leans on: the measure closes as
     * his shoulder comes down, and the last lines stop where his feet are. `shape-outside`
     * is what buys that, and it needs a float — which is why this element is first in the
     * section and empty: a float only pushes what comes after it.
     *
     * THE POLYGON IS THE SILHOUETTE'S OWN LEFT EDGE, traced off dummy-leaning.webp rather
     * than drawn by eye: the artwork's leftmost painted pixel was read every two rows, hulled,
     * and simplified to the seven vertices below. The drawing crosses that line by at most
     * 11 of its own 953 pixels — under 6 on screen — and `shape-margin` is more than an order
     * of magnitude larger, so no line of text ever touches him. Retrace both if the artwork
     * is ever recropped.
     *
     * NOT `shape-outside: url()`, which would follow the alpha exactly and let a line of text
     * into the gap under his crossed arms and between his legs. Text in an armpit is not a
     * design, it is a bug that looks deliberate.
     *
     * THE NEGATIVE MARGIN IS WHAT PUTS HIM MOSTLY OUTSIDE THE MEASURE. The prose is capped at
     * 56rem inside a 72rem shell, which leaves a 224-pixel column that no text can reach; he
     * hangs in it, and only his lower half — where the lean has carried him left — comes back
     * over the measure. `border-box` on the shape is not decoration: a negative margin shrinks
     * the margin box, which is what the percentages would otherwise be resolved against.
     *
     * HIS FEET ARE ON THE FOOTER'S RULE, not on the last line of the text, and that is what
     * decides his height rather than any judgement about size. From the top of the heading to
     * that rule is 626 pixels, measured; he is 626 tall and stands at the top of the text, so
     * the page reads as something he is leaning at the end of. Anchored to the prose instead —
     * which is what the first version did — he ended a hundred pixels short of the line, and
     * looked like a drawing that had been dropped in the margin.
     *
     * The 64 pixels he overflows his own section by are the gap above the footer, which holds
     * nothing. A float overflows without pushing anything, and the footer clears nothing.
     *
     * THE 18 PIXELS DOWN ARE ONE FOOT, NOT A NUDGE. The two feet do not end level: in the
     * artwork the near one reaches row 952 and the far one stops at 925, and a figure whose
     * lowest ink is flush with the rule is therefore standing on its near foot alone, with the
     * other hanging above the line. Dropped by the difference between them — 27 of the
     * drawing's rows, 18 at the size it is drawn — the far foot lands on the rule and the near
     * one crosses it, which is what standing on a step looks like. Re-measure both if the
     * artwork is ever recropped.
     *
     * AND IT IS `top`, NOT A MARGIN, WHICH IS NOT A DETAIL. A margin moves the float's box, and
     * a box reaching into the footer's own band is a box the footer will not pass under: the
     * footer establishes its own formatting context, so it was laid out *beside* the float
     * instead — narrowed to what was left, then re-centred inside it by its own `margin: 0
     * auto`. The whole footer shifted 515 pixels left and its rule stopped short of the
     * drawing. Relative positioning moves what is painted and leaves the box where the layout
     * put it, which is the half everything else depends on.
     *
     * The exclusion therefore keeps the box's position, 18 pixels above the ink it was traced
     * from. That is the harmless direction: the silhouette leans left as it descends, so the
     * shape at any height is further left than the drawing is there, and the text stops that
     * much sooner rather than that much later.
     */
    .mascot {
        display: none;
    }

    /*
     * Drawn only where the free column exists, which is the same width at which the shell
     * stops growing. Below it the drawing would be over the prose rather than beside it, and
     * a float with a shape in a narrow measure turns a paragraph into a staircase.
     */
    @media (min-width: 75rem) {
        /*
         * THE TWO LOCALES ARE MADE THE SAME HEIGHT, and that is what lets one figure stand on
         * the rule in both. The French block runs 562 pixels and the English one 507, so the
         * footer's rule sits 55 pixels higher in English — and a figure tall enough to reach it
         * in French would cross it there, into the row that carries the copyright.
         *
         * The floor is the taller of the two, measured. It costs the English page 55 pixels of
         * air under its links, which is the price of the two pages ending on the same line;
         * re-measure it if either text is rewritten, since nothing here can know that it moved.
         */
        .about {
            min-height: 562px;
        }

        .mascot {
            display: block;
            float: right;
            width: 317px;
            height: 626px;
            position: relative;
            top: 18px;
            margin-right: -224px;
            shape-outside: polygon(
                    71% 0%,
                    100% 0%,
                    100% 100%,
                    16% 100%,
                    4% 99%,
                    0% 97%,
                    44% 12%,
                    55% 4%,
                    63% 1%
                )
                border-box;
            shape-margin: 1rem;
            background: url('/dummy-leaning.webp') no-repeat center / contain;
        }
    }

    h2 {
        font-size: var(--jd-text-xl);
        line-height: var(--jd-line-tight);
        margin: 0 0 var(--jd-space-6);
    }

    p {
        margin: 0 0 var(--jd-space-4);
    }

    .links {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        gap: var(--jd-space-3);
        list-style: none;
        margin: var(--jd-space-2) 0 0;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .links a {
        display: inline-block;
        border: 1px solid var(--jd-border);
        border-radius: var(--jd-radius-sm);
        background: var(--jd-surface);
        padding: var(--jd-space-2) var(--jd-space-4);
        color: var(--jd-text-primary);
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: var(--jd-text-sm);
    }

    .links a:hover {
        border-color: var(--jd-accent);
    }
</style>
